StudyData.addIndex(7, {"english":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Poorvi"],"note":"Grammar and writing, plus every lesson of Poorvi (summarised in our own words).","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"the-day-the-river-spoke","title":"The Day the River Spoke","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A story extract in which a young village girl, Jahnavi, who longs to go to school, talks to the River. The River’s gentle advice gives her the courage to sit in the school and listen, and the teacher and her parents then let her study.","nTerms":5},{"id":"try-again","title":"Try Again","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A narrative poem about King Bruce of Scotland, who is ready to give up after repeated failures until he watches a spider try nine times to climb to its web, and succeed on the next try. The king takes heart and tries again.","nTerms":5},{"id":"three-days-to-see","title":"Three Days to See","section":"Poorvi","summary":"An essay by Helen Keller, who was deaf and blind, imagining how she would use three days of sight. She would see her friends on the first day, the dawn and the museums on the second, and the busy city on the third, and she urges everyone to value their senses.","nTerms":6},{"id":"animals-birds-and-dr-dolittle","title":"Animals, Birds, and Dr. Dolittle","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A fantasy story in which kindly John Dolittle learns animal languages from his parrot Polynesia, gives up being a doctor for people, and becomes famous among animals everywhere as the doctor who understands them.","nTerms":5},{"id":"a-funny-man","title":"A Funny Man","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A light-hearted nonsense poem in which a funny man appears on the street, wearing a shoe on his head and hats on his feet, offers the speaker a currant bun as a ‘rose’, and hops home on his head.","nTerms":5},{"id":"say-the-right-thing","title":"Say the Right Thing","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A humorous one-act play in which Mrs. Shaw tells her talkative daughter Mary to say kind and pleasant things to the visitors. But Mary forgets, insults the guests’ children, friends, dog and relatives without knowing it, and even at the end says the wrong thing.","nTerms":5},{"id":"my-brothers-great-invention","title":"My Brother’s Great Invention","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A humorous story told by fourteen-year-old Anita about her younger brother Anand, who thinks he is a scientist. His burglar alarm drenches their father, and his time machine seems to swallow a real thief, Boppa, one night when their parents are away.","nTerms":6},{"id":"paper-boats","title":"Paper Boats","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A poem in which a child floats paper boats down a stream every day, writing his name and village on them in the hope that someone far away will find them, and dreams that fairies of sleep sail in them carrying baskets full of dreams.","nTerms":5},{"id":"north-south-east-west","title":"North, South, East, West","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A set of postcards written by Shaana, a girl from Rameswaram island, to her classmates while she travels around India with her parents, from the glaciers of Kashmir to forests, wetlands, deserts, rivers and beaches, before she returns home.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-tunnel","title":"The Tunnel","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A story set in a jungle, where a boy named Suraj befriends Sunder Singh, the watchman of a railway tunnel. One night they drive a leopard out of the tunnel just before the night mail comes, and Suraj later sees the watchman’s lamp from the train.","nTerms":5},{"id":"travel","title":"Travel","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A short poem in which the speaker hears and sees trains night and day although the railroad is miles away. In spite of being happy with her friends, the speaker would take any train, no matter where it goes.","nTerms":5},{"id":"conquering-the-summit","title":"Conquering the Summit","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A true account of Arunima Sinha, a national-level volleyball player who lost a leg in a train accident in 2011 but fulfilled her vow to climb Mount Everest in 2013, becoming the first Indian female amputee to do so, and later completed the Seven Summits.","nTerms":6},{"id":"a-homage-to-our-brave-soldiers","title":"A Homage to Our Brave Soldiers","section":"Poorvi","summary":"Two friends exchange letters. Soumya writes about her school visit to the National War Memorial in New Delhi, its design, the Amar Jawan Jyoti, the gallantry awards and the stories of heroes. Ananda replies with his own feelings of gratitude and a poem.","nTerms":6},{"id":"my-dear-soldiers","title":"My Dear Soldiers","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A short poem of tribute to soldiers by Dr. A. P. J. Abdul Kalam. It praises them as the great sons and daughters of the land who guard the borders in every season and terrain, and prays for their well-being.","nTerms":5},{"id":"rani-abbakka","title":"Rani Abbakka","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A graphic story about Rani Abbakka of Ullal in coastal Karnataka, who refused to pay tribute to the Portuguese, built up trade and an alliance, and fought them bravely until her small kingdom was free.","nTerms":6},{"id":"adverbs","title":"Adverbs","section":"Grammar","summary":"An adverb tells us more about a verb, an adjective or another adverb. This chapter covers the kinds of adverbs, how to form them from adjectives, and where to place them in a sentence.","nTerms":5},{"id":"perfect-tenses","title":"Perfect Tenses","section":"Grammar","summary":"Perfect tenses link one time to another: a past action with the present, or an action that happened before another past or future time. This chapter covers the present, past and future perfect tenses.","nTerms":5},{"id":"modals","title":"Modals","section":"Grammar","summary":"Modal verbs such as can, may, must and should show ability, permission, possibility, obligation and advice. This chapter explains what each modal means and how to use it correctly.","nTerms":5},{"id":"conjunctions","title":"Conjunctions","section":"Grammar","summary":"Conjunctions join words, phrases and sentences. This chapter explains coordinating and subordinating conjunctions and how to use them to make longer, smoother sentences.","nTerms":5},{"id":"subject-verb-agreement","title":"Subject–Verb Agreement","section":"Grammar","summary":"The verb must agree with its subject in number: a singular subject takes a singular verb, and a plural subject takes a plural verb. This chapter covers the common rules and tricky cases.","nTerms":5},{"id":"direct-and-indirect-speech","title":"Direct and Indirect Speech","section":"Grammar","summary":"Direct speech gives the exact words a person said, inside quotation marks. Indirect (reported) speech tells what they said without quotes. This chapter covers statements and questions and the changes needed.","nTerms":5},{"id":"formal-letter","title":"Formal Letter","section":"Writing Skills","summary":"A formal letter is written to a person in authority or to an organisation, such as a principal, an editor or a shop manager. This chapter explains its layout, the polite language to use, and the correct closing.","nTerms":5},{"id":"diary-entry","title":"Diary Entry","section":"Writing Skills","summary":"A diary entry is a personal record of the day’s events and feelings. This chapter explains the format of a diary entry, the tense to use, and how to write honestly and vividly.","nTerms":5},{"id":"story-writing","title":"Story Writing","section":"Writing Skills","summary":"A good story has a clear beginning, middle and end, interesting characters and a problem to solve. This chapter explains the parts of a story, how to write dialogue and how to finish with a satisfying ending.","nTerms":5},{"id":"message-writing","title":"Message Writing","section":"Writing Skills","summary":"A message is a short note that passes on important information to someone who is not there. This chapter explains the details a good message must include and how to write one clearly and politely.","nTerms":5}]},"englishpb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["English (PSEB, Class 7)"],"note":"Follows the PSEB 2026–27 syllabus; lessons summarised in our own words from the PSEB textbook (2023–24 edition).","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"pb-nouns-pronouns","title":"Nouns and Pronouns","section":"Grammar","summary":"A noun names a person, place, thing or idea; a pronoun stands in for a noun. This chapter revises nouns and pronouns and adds the main kinds of each.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-verbs-tenses","title":"Verbs and Tenses","section":"Grammar","summary":"A verb shows an action or state. Tense shows the time. This chapter covers simple, continuous and perfect forms in the present and past.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-adjectives","title":"Adjectives and Degrees of Comparison","section":"Grammar","summary":"An adjective describes a noun or pronoun. Most adjectives have three degrees: positive, comparative and superlative.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-adverbs","title":"Adverbs","section":"Grammar","summary":"An adverb tells more about a verb, an adjective or another adverb. It answers how, when, where or how much.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-prepositions","title":"Prepositions","section":"Grammar","summary":"A preposition shows how a noun or pronoun is related to another word in the sentence, in time, place or direction. This chapter also separates easily confused pairs.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-conjunctions","title":"Conjunctions","section":"Grammar","summary":"A conjunction joins words, phrases or sentences. This chapter covers the common co-ordinating and subordinating conjunctions.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-articles-determiners","title":"Articles and Determiners","section":"Grammar","summary":"Articles (a, an, the) are the commonest determiners. Determiners are words placed before a noun to show which one or how many.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-sentences","title":"Sentences: Kinds and Subject–Predicate","section":"Grammar","summary":"A sentence is a group of words that makes complete sense. This chapter covers the four kinds of sentences and the subject and predicate.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-word-power","title":"Word Power","section":"Vocabulary","summary":"Building vocabulary: prefixes and suffixes, antonyms, unscrambling jumbled words, comparisons with “as … as”, alphabetical order and the use of “instead of”.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-letters-applications","title":"Letters and Applications","section":"Writing","summary":"How to lay out and write a personal letter, a formal letter and a school application.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-paragraph-notice-message","title":"Paragraph, Notice and Message","section":"Writing","summary":"How to write a clear paragraph, a school notice and a short message.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-story-picture","title":"Story Writing and Picture Description","section":"Writing","summary":"How to plan and write a short story and how to describe a picture in good order.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-rent-for-water","title":"Rent for Water","section":"Lessons","summary":"A short tale in which Tenali Raman settles a quarrel between a farmer and his greedy neighbour over a well that was sold without its water.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-trains","title":"Trains","section":"Lessons","summary":"A short, rhythmic poem by James S. Tippett that follows trains as they rush across mountains, plains and rivers, bringing passengers, mail and goods safely to their stations.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-birbals-khichdi","title":"Birbal's Khichdi","section":"Lessons","summary":"On a freezing winter night Akbar cheats a poor brahmin of his reward, and Birbal shows him his unfairness by cooking khichdi a yard above the fire.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-glass-of-milk","title":"A Glass of Milk","section":"Lessons","summary":"A three-scene play, adapted by Vandana Lunyal, about a poor book-seller named Howard Kelly, the woman who gave him milk, and how he repaid her kindness years later as a doctor.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-hunter-and-deer","title":"The Hunter and the Deer","section":"Lessons","summary":"An anonymous poem in which a hunter, ready to shoot a buck, is moved by the animal's plea and its family, and goes home happy after befriending the deer.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-mountaineers","title":"Mountaineers","section":"Lessons","summary":"An informative lesson on climbing Mount Everest: the first climbers, the first Indian to reach the top in 1965, and the dangers of the “death zone”.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-princess-never-smiled","title":"The Princess Who Never Smiled","section":"Lessons","summary":"A Russian tale told as a play-style story: a kind boy named Ivan, helped by a fish, a mouse and a grasshopper, finally makes the sad Princess Tanya laugh.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-an-earthquake","title":"An Earthquake","section":"Lessons","summary":"A short poem by Vandana Lunyal in which the speaker lies awake as a quake shakes the bed and then sees the town, houses and trees reduced to rubble.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-treasure-hunt","title":"A Treasure Hunt","section":"Lessons","summary":"Sagar finds a treasure map in an old book and gathers a lion, eagle, sheep, camel and turtle on the way, only to learn from an owl that friendship is the real treasure.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-hole-in-fence","title":"A Hole in the Fence","section":"Lessons","summary":"A short moral tale about a boy with a bad temper, a father who sets him a task with nails, and the holes left in a fence.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-naughty-boy","title":"There was a Naughty Boy","section":"Lessons","summary":"A light, humorous poem by John Keats about a boy who runs away from England to Scotland and finds everything there much the same as at home.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-hachiko","title":"Hachiko – The World's Most Loyal Dog","section":"Lessons","summary":"The true story of Hachiko, the Akita dog who went to Shibuya station in Tokyo at the same time every day to wait for his dead master.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-gift-for-sidhak","title":"A Gift for Sidhak","section":"Lessons","summary":"On Republic Day, nine-year-old Sidhak, who wanted a toy kitchen set, sees a woman fighter pilot on TV and dreams of flying jets, with her grandfather's support.","nTerms":4}]},"hindi":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Malhar"],"note":"व्याकरण और लेखन, साथ में मल्हार के सभी पाठ (अपने शब्दों में)।","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"visheshan","title":"विशेषण","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"जो शब्द संज्ञा या सर्वनाम की विशेषता बताते हैं, उन्हें विशेषण कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में विशेषण के भेद और विशेष्य से उसका संबंध सरल उदाहरणों के साथ समझाया गया है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kriya","title":"क्रिया","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"क्रिया वह शब्द है जिससे किसी काम का करना या होना पता चले। इस अध्याय में क्रिया के भेद – सकर्मक, अकर्मक और धातु – सरल रूप में समझाए गए हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kaal","title":"काल","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"क्रिया के जिस रूप से काम के होने का समय पता चले, उसे काल कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में भूतकाल, वर्तमानकाल और भविष्यत्काल तथा उनके भेद उदाहरण सहित दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":4},{"id":"karak","title":"कारक","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"संज्ञा या सर्वनाम का क्रिया या दूसरे शब्दों से जो संबंध होता है, उसे कारक कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में आठ कारक, उनकी विभक्तियाँ (चिह्न) और उदाहरण दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"paryayvachi-shabd","title":"पर्यायवाची शब्द","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"समान अर्थ वाले अलग-अलग शब्दों को पर्यायवाची शब्द कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में प्रकृति, परिवार और दैनिक जीवन से जुड़े प्रमुख पर्यायवाची शब्द दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"muhavare","title":"मुहावरे","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"मुहावरे ऐसे वाक्यांश हैं जो अपने सामान्य अर्थ को छोड़कर विशेष अर्थ बताते हैं। इस अध्याय में प्रचलित मुहावरे, उनके अर्थ और वाक्य-प्रयोग दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":4},{"id":"aupcharik-patra","title":"औपचारिक पत्र","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"औपचारिक पत्र प्रधानाचार्य, अधिकारी या संपादक जैसे व्यक्तियों को लिखे जाते हैं। इस अध्याय में पत्र का प्रारूप, भाषा और अवकाश-प्रार्थना पत्र का नमूना दिया गया है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"samvad-lekhan","title":"संवाद लेखन","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"दो या अधिक व्यक्तियों की आपसी बातचीत को लिखित रूप देना संवाद लेखन कहलाता है। इस अध्याय में संवाद लिखने के नियम और एक नमूना संवाद दिया गया है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"nibandh-lekhan","title":"निबंध लेखन","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"किसी विषय पर अपने विचारों को क्रम से लिखना निबंध है। इस अध्याय में निबंध के भाग, लिखने की विधि और एक नमूना रूपरेखा दी गई है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"malhar-maa-kah-ek-kahani","title":"माँ, कह एक कहानी","section":"मल्हार","summary":"मैथिलीशरण गुप्त की यह कविता 'यशोधरा' काव्य का अंश है। इसमें माँ यशोधरा अपने बेटे राहुल को उसके पिता सिद्धार्थ से जुड़ी एक घायल हंस की कहानी सुनाती हैं और अंत में निर्णय राहुल पर छोड़ देती हैं। कविता करुणा और न्याय का संबंध समझाती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-teen-buddhimaan","title":"तीन बुद्धिमान","section":"मल्हार","summary":"यह एक लोककथा है। एक निर्धन पिता अपने तीन बेटों को पैनी दृष्टि और तीव्र बुद्धि का धन जोड़ने की सीख देता है। वही बुद्धि बेटों को खोए हुए ऊँट की पहचान बताने में काम आती है और अंत में राजा उनकी प्रतिभा से प्रभावित होकर उन्हें दरबार में रख लेता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-phool-aur-kaanta","title":"फूल और काँटा","section":"मल्हार","summary":"अयोध्यासिंह उपाध्याय 'हरिऔध' की इस कविता में फूल और काँटे की तुलना है। दोनों एक ही पौधे पर, एक-सी चाँदनी, वर्षा और हवा में पलते हैं, फिर भी उनका स्वभाव अलग होता है। कवि बताता है कि मनुष्य की महानता उसके कुल से नहीं, उसके कर्म और व्यवहार से तय होती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-paani-re-paani","title":"पानी रे पानी","section":"मल्हार","summary":"अनुपम मिश्र के इस निबंध में बताया गया है कि कभी पानी की कमी (अकाल) और कभी पानी की अधिकता (बाढ़) एक ही सिक्के के दो पहलू हैं। लेखक धरती को एक बड़ी गुल्लक बताकर समझाता है कि तालाब और झीलें वर्षा का जल भूजल के रूप में जमा करती हैं, और उन्हें बचाना ज़रूरी है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-nahin-hona-beemaar","title":"नहीं होना बीमार","section":"मल्हार","summary":"स्वयं प्रकाश की इस हास्य-कहानी में एक बच्चा अस्पताल में मरीज़ों की देखभाल देखकर सोचता है कि बीमार होना मज़ेदार है। स्कूल से बचने के लिए वह बीमारी का बहाना बनाता है, पर कड़वी दवा, भूख और बोरियत झेलकर तय करता है कि फिर कभी बहाना नहीं बनाएगा।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-giridhar-kaviray-ki-kundaliyan","title":"गिरिधर कविराय की कुंडलियाँ","section":"मल्हार","summary":"इस पाठ में गिरिधर कविराय की दो नीतिपरक कुंडलियाँ हैं। पहली सिखाती है कि बिना सोचे-विचारे काम करने पर पछताना पड़ता है और दूसरी कहती है कि बीती बात भूलकर आगे की सुध लेनी चाहिए। सरल भाषा के कारण ये पंक्तियाँ कहावत की तरह प्रचलित हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-varsha-bahar","title":"वर्षा-बहार","section":"मल्हार","summary":"मुकुटधर पांडेय की इस कविता में वर्षा ऋतु की बहार का सुंदर चित्र है। बिजली, बादल, झरने, ठंडी हवा, नाचते मोर, गाते मेंढक और खिले गुलाब मिलकर वर्षा की शोभा बनाते हैं। कवि कहता है कि संसार की शोभा इसी ऋतु पर निर्भर है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-birju-maharaj-se-saakshatkar","title":"बिरजू महाराज से साक्षात्कार","section":"मल्हार","summary":"यह कथक के महान नर्तक पद्मविभूषण बिरजू महाराज से बच्चों की बातचीत है। उन्होंने अपने संघर्ष भरे बचपन, कथक की परंपरा, लय के महत्त्व, लोक और शास्त्रीय नृत्य के अंतर तथा अपने शौकों के बारे में बताया है। वे बच्चों को हुनर सीखने और संगीत से जुड़ने की प्रेरणा देते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-chidiya","title":"चिड़िया","section":"मल्हार","summary":"आरसी प्रसाद सिंह की इस कविता में चिड़िया मनुष्य को प्रेम, मिल-जुलकर रहने और संतोष का संदेश देती है। पक्षी लोभ नहीं करते, अपने श्रम का ही लेते हैं और स्वतंत्र आकाश में निर्भय उड़ते हैं। कवि मनुष्य से कहता है कि वह द्रोह छोड़कर मानवता अपनाए।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-meera-ke-pad","title":"मीरा के पद","section":"मल्हार","summary":"इस पाठ में कृष्णभक्त कवयित्री मीरा के दो पद हैं। पहले पद में वे श्रीकृष्ण से अपने नेत्रों में बसने की प्रार्थना करती हैं और उनके मोहक रूप का वर्णन करती हैं। दूसरे पद में सावन की वर्षा का आनंद और हरि के आने की आहट का भाव है।","nTerms":5}]},"hindipb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["आओ हिंदी सीखें-7 (द्वितीय भाषा)"],"note":"व्याकरण और लेखन, तथा पुस्तक 'आओ हिंदी सीखें-7' के सभी पाठ (अपने शब्दों में सार)।","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"visheshan","title":"विशेषण","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"जो शब्द संज्ञा या सर्वनाम की विशेषता बताते हैं, उन्हें विशेषण कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में विशेषण के भेद और विशेष्य से उसका संबंध सरल उदाहरणों के साथ समझाया गया है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kriya","title":"क्रिया","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"क्रिया वह शब्द है जिससे किसी काम का करना या होना पता चले। इस अध्याय में क्रिया के भेद – सकर्मक, अकर्मक और धातु – सरल रूप में समझाए गए हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kaal","title":"काल","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"क्रिया के जिस रूप से काम के होने का समय पता चले, उसे काल कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में भूतकाल, वर्तमानकाल और भविष्यत्काल तथा उनके भेद उदाहरण सहित दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":4},{"id":"karak","title":"कारक","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"संज्ञा या सर्वनाम का क्रिया या दूसरे शब्दों से जो संबंध होता है, उसे कारक कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में आठ कारक, उनकी विभक्तियाँ (चिह्न) और उदाहरण दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"paryayvachi-shabd","title":"पर्यायवाची शब्द","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"समान अर्थ वाले अलग-अलग शब्दों को पर्यायवाची शब्द कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में प्रकृति, परिवार और दैनिक जीवन से जुड़े प्रमुख पर्यायवाची शब्द दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"muhavare","title":"मुहावरे","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"मुहावरे ऐसे वाक्यांश हैं जो अपने सामान्य अर्थ को छोड़कर विशेष अर्थ बताते हैं। इस अध्याय में प्रचलित मुहावरे, उनके अर्थ और वाक्य-प्रयोग दिए गए हैं।","nTerms":4},{"id":"aupcharik-patra","title":"औपचारिक पत्र","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"औपचारिक पत्र प्रधानाचार्य, अधिकारी या संपादक जैसे व्यक्तियों को लिखे जाते हैं। इस अध्याय में पत्र का प्रारूप, भाषा और अवकाश-प्रार्थना पत्र का नमूना दिया गया है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"samvad-lekhan","title":"संवाद लेखन","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"दो या अधिक व्यक्तियों की आपसी बातचीत को लिखित रूप देना संवाद लेखन कहलाता है। इस अध्याय में संवाद लिखने के नियम और एक नमूना संवाद दिया गया है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"nibandh-lekhan","title":"निबंध लेखन","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"किसी विषय पर अपने विचारों को क्रम से लिखना निबंध है। इस अध्याय में निबंध के भाग, लिखने की विधि और एक नमूना रूपरेखा दी गई है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"bharat-ke-kone-kone-se","title":"भारत के कोने-कोने से","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में देश के कोने-कोने से आए बच्चे नई उमंग और आशा का संदेश लाते हैं। वे पहाड़ की ऊँचाई, सागर की गहराई और गंगा-यमुना का निर्मल जल लाते हैं और देश का नाम और ऊँचा करने का संकल्प लेते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"parmatma-jo-karta-hai","title":"परमात्मा जो करता है, अच्छा ही करता है (संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"राजा शूरसेन का मंत्री सुमति हर घटना में यही कहता था कि जो होता है, अच्छे के लिए होता है। राजा उसकी बात से नाराज़ होकर उसे कुएँ में गिरा देता है, पर बाद में उसी बात की सच्चाई समझता है और मंत्री से क्षमा माँगता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"raktdan-ek-bahumulya-sanskar","title":"रक्तदान – एक बहुमूल्य संस्कार (महेश कुमार शर्मा)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस लेख में बताया गया है कि रक्तदान सबसे बड़ा दान है क्योंकि इससे किसी को जीवन मिलता है। लेख रक्तदान से जुड़े डर और भ्रम दूर करता है, रक्त-समूहों की जानकारी देता है और बताता है कि किसे रक्तदान नहीं करना चाहिए।","nTerms":5},{"id":"phool-aur-kanta","title":"फूल और काँटा (अयोध्यासिंह उपाध्याय 'हरिऔध')","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"कविता में कवि बताता है कि फूल और काँटा एक ही पौधे पर, एक जैसी परिस्थितियों में जन्म लेते हैं, फिर भी उनके स्वभाव अलग होते हैं। काँटा चुभकर दुख देता है, फूल सुगंध और रंग से सबको सुख देता है। इसलिए बड़प्पन कुल से नहीं, गुणों से मिलता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"haar-ki-jeet","title":"हार की जीत (सुदर्शन)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"बाबा भारती को अपना घोड़ा सुलतान बहुत प्रिय था। डाकू खड्गसिंह अपाहिज बनकर उसे छल से ले जाता है। बाबा उससे केवल यह प्रार्थना करते हैं कि यह बात किसी को न बताए, ताकि लोग गरीबों और ज़रूरतमंदों पर विश्वास करना न छोड़ दें। यह सुनकर डाकू का हृदय बदल जाता है और वह रात में घोड़ा लौटा जाता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"rashtra-ke-gaurav-prateek","title":"राष्ट्र के गौरव प्रतीक (सुधा जैन 'सुदीप')","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह पाठ अध्यापक और विद्यार्थियों की बातचीत के रूप में है। स्कूल की प्रदर्शनी देखते हुए बच्चे राष्ट्रीय ध्वज, राष्ट्रगान, राष्ट्रगीत, राष्ट्रीय चिह्न, राष्ट्रीय पशु-पक्षी आदि के बारे में जानते हैं और इनका सम्मान करने का संकल्प लेते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"aa-ri-barkha","title":"आ री बरखा (डॉ० राकेश कुमार बब्बर)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में कवि तपती धरती पर बरखा से आने की विनती करता है। गर्मी से पेड़ सूख गए हैं, पक्षी चुप हैं, फूल मुरझा गए हैं और नदियाँ सूख रही हैं। बच्चे और किसान दोनों वर्षा की राह देख रहे हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"vijay-diwas","title":"विजय दिवस (डॉ० मीनाक्षी वर्मा)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"बारह वर्ष का हार्दिक अपने शहीद पिता कैप्टन वैभव शुक्ला की याद में पले-बढ़े परिवार के साथ चंडीगढ़ आता है। दादा जी एस.एस.पी. हैं। नए स्कूल में वह दादा जी के नाम का रौब जमाता है। कारगिल विजय दिवस के कार्यक्रम में पिता के बलिदान और दादा जी की ईमानदारी के बारे में सुनकर वह शर्मिंदा होता है और सबसे माफ़ी माँगने का संकल्प करता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"swarajya-ki-neenv","title":"स्वराज्य की नींव (शिव शंकर)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह एकांकी 1857 के समय की झाँसी की रानी लक्ष्मीबाई के साहस पर आधारित है। अंग्रेज़ झाँसी पर अधिकार करना चाहते हैं, पर रानी पेंशन लेने से मना करके स्वराज्य के लिए लड़ने का निश्चय करती है। उसके साथी, सहेलियाँ और सैनिक मिलकर संघर्ष करते हैं और रानी का बलिदान स्वराज्य की नींव बन जाता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"badhe-chalo-badhe-chalo","title":"बढ़े चलो, बढ़े चलो (सोहनलाल द्विवेदी)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह जोश भरी कविता देशवासियों को हर कठिनाई में निडर होकर आगे बढ़ने की प्रेरणा देती है। कवि कहता है कि शस्त्र, भोजन या वस्त्र न भी हों, फिर भी रुको नहीं, झुको नहीं, डरो नहीं और स्वतंत्रता का मोल चुकाने के लिए आगे बढ़ते चलो।","nTerms":5},{"id":"dhira-ki-hoshiyari","title":"धीरा की होशियारी (कुछ और कहानियाँ में से)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"जूते पॉलिश करने वाला मेहनती लड़का धीरा सुनता है कि जौहरी की दुकान से सोने का कंठा चुराकर कोई भाग गया है। उसी समय एक ग्राहक उससे जूते पॉलिश करवाने आता है। धीरा की सूझबूझ और होशियारी से असली चोर पकड़ा जाता है और उसे इनाम मिलता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"ashok-ka-shastra-tyag","title":"अशोक का शस्त्र-त्याग (संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह एकांकी सम्राट अशोक के कलिंग-युद्ध के बाद हुए हृदय-परिवर्तन पर आधारित है। लंबे युद्ध के बाद जब कलिंग की राजकुमारी पद्मा स्त्रियों की सेना लेकर सामने आती है, तो अशोक का मन पिघल जाता है। वह हथियार डालकर अहिंसा और बौद्ध धर्म की शरण लेता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"saksharta-abhiyan","title":"साक्षरता अभियान (डॉ० कमलेश बंसल)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में कवयित्री छोटे-छोटे उदाहरणों से समझाती हैं कि थोड़ी-थोड़ी मेहनत से बड़ा काम हो जाता है। चिड़िया तिनका-तिनका जोड़कर घोंसला बनाती है, बूँद-बूँद से घड़ा भरता है, उसी तरह रोज़ थोड़ा-थोड़ा पढ़ने से हम ज्ञानी बन सकते हैं। वे सबको साक्षर बनाने का संदेश देती हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"gillu","title":"गिल्लू (महादेवी वर्मा)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह महादेवी वर्मा का एक संस्मरण है। लेखिका घायल गिलहरी के बच्चे को बचाकर घर लाती हैं, उसका उपचार करती हैं और उसका नाम गिल्लू रखती हैं। गिल्लू अपनी शरारतों और स्नेह से परिवार का सदस्य बन जाता है। उसकी मृत्यु पर लेखिका उसे सोनजुही की लता के नीचे समाधि देती हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"dharamshala","title":"धर्मशाला (डॉ० मीनाक्षी वर्मा)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह पाठ सामिषा की डायरी के रूप में लिखा गया यात्रा-वर्णन है। वह परिवार के साथ हिमाचल प्रदेश के धर्मशाला और आसपास के स्थान देखती है और वहाँ की प्रकृति, संस्कृति और इतिहास का वर्णन करती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"koi-nahin-begana","title":"कोई नहीं बेगाना (डॉ० योगेन्द्र बख्शी)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में आनंदपुर साहिब के युद्ध का वर्णन है। भाई कन्हैया तपती दोपहर में कंधे पर मशक उठाकर सभी घायलों को पानी पिलाते हैं, चाहे वे मित्र हों या शत्रु। शिकायत होने पर वे गुरु जी को उत्तर देते हैं कि उन्हें हर प्राणी में प्रभु का रूप दिखता है। गुरु जी प्रसन्न होकर उन्हें मरहम देते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"anyay-ke-virodh-mein","title":"अन्याय के विरोध में (अंतोन चेखव)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"एक लेखक अपने बच्चों की गवर्नेस जूलिया की तनख्वाह के हिसाब में तरह-तरह की कटौतियाँ गिनाकर उसे बहुत कम पैसे देता है। जूलिया चुपचाप सब सह लेती है और उलटे धन्यवाद देती है। तब लेखक बताता है कि यह उसका मज़ाक था और उसे पूरे पैसे देकर सीख देता है कि अन्याय को चुपचाप सहना गलत है, उसका विरोध करना चाहिए।","nTerms":5},{"id":"sadak-suraksha-jeevan-raksha","title":"सड़क सुरक्षा – जीवन रक्षा (डॉ० सुनील बहल)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस संवाद-पाठ में स्कूल की प्रार्थना सभा में एक ट्रैफ़िक पुलिस अधिकारी विद्यार्थियों के प्रश्नों के उत्तर देते हुए सड़क सुरक्षा के नियम, यातायात-चिह्न और सावधानियाँ समझाते हैं। संदेश है कि स्वयं सुरक्षित रहें और दूसरों की सुरक्षा का भी ध्यान रखें।","nTerms":5},{"id":"dohavali","title":"दोहावली (संत कबीर)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस पाठ में संत कबीर के नौ दोहे हैं, जो मीठी वाणी, समय के सदुपयोग, परिश्रम, प्रेम, सोच-समझकर बोलने, विनम्रता, निंदक के महत्त्व, आत्म-निरीक्षण और संयम की शिक्षा देते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"main-jeeti","title":"मैं जीती (कुछ और कहानियाँ में से)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"अनुराधा लंगड़ाकर चलती है और नए स्कूल में अकेलापन और अपमान महसूस करती है। माँ के प्रोत्साहन से वह हिम्मत नहीं हारती। तरणताल पर वह एक डूबती लड़की कला को बचाती है, जो उसकी सहपाठी माला की बहन निकलती है। इसके बाद सब उसके मित्र बन जाते हैं।","nTerms":5}]},"maths":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Ganita Prakash Part I","Ganita Prakash Part II"],"reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"large-numbers-around-us","title":"Large Numbers Around Us","section":"Part I","summary":"We read, write, compare and estimate very large numbers using lakhs and crores, and meet the international system of millions and billions.","nTerms":4},{"id":"arithmetic-expressions","title":"Arithmetic Expressions","section":"Part I","summary":"We read and compare arithmetic expressions, use brackets and the order of operations, and use properties of numbers to calculate smartly.","nTerms":4},{"id":"a-peek-beyond-the-point","title":"A Peek Beyond the Point","section":"Part I","summary":"We extend place value beyond the decimal point to tenths, hundredths and thousandths, and compare, add and subtract decimal numbers.","nTerms":4},{"id":"expressions-using-letter-numbers","title":"Expressions using Letter-Numbers","section":"Part I","summary":"Letters can stand for numbers that vary or are unknown. We write rules from patterns as algebraic expressions, simplify them and find their values.","nTerms":4},{"id":"parallel-and-intersecting-lines","title":"Parallel and Intersecting Lines","section":"Part I","summary":"We study the angles made when lines cross, and when a transversal cuts parallel lines, and use these relations to find unknown angles.","nTerms":4},{"id":"number-play","title":"Number Play","section":"Part I","summary":"We explore even and odd numbers, digit puzzles and divisibility tests, and use them to explain why number tricks work.","nTerms":4},{"id":"a-tale-of-three-intersecting-lines","title":"A Tale of Three Intersecting Lines","section":"Part I","summary":"Three lines that intersect pairwise form a triangle. We study its angle sum, exterior angles and the condition on side lengths for a triangle to exist.","nTerms":4},{"id":"working-with-fractions","title":"Working with Fractions","section":"Part I","summary":"We multiply and divide fractions, find a fraction of a quantity, use reciprocals, and solve word problems with fractions and mixed numbers.","nTerms":4},{"id":"geometric-twins","title":"Geometric Twins","section":"Part II","summary":"Figures that have exactly the same shape and size are congruent. We study congruent plane figures and the conditions under which two triangles are congruent.","nTerms":4},{"id":"operations-with-integers","title":"Operations with Integers","section":"Part II","summary":"We multiply and divide integers, learn the sign rules and see how the properties of operations extend to negative numbers.","nTerms":4},{"id":"finding-common-ground","title":"Finding Common Ground","section":"Part II","summary":"We study factors, multiples and prime factorisation, and use them to find the HCF and LCM of numbers and to solve everyday problems.","nTerms":5},{"id":"another-peek-beyond-the-point","title":"Another Peek Beyond the Point","section":"Part II","summary":"We multiply and divide decimal numbers, including by powers of 10, and use these operations in measurements and money problems.","nTerms":4},{"id":"connecting-the-dots","title":"Connecting the Dots…","section":"Part II","summary":"We summarise data with the arithmetic mean, median and mode, find the range and read what each measure tells us about the data.","nTerms":4},{"id":"constructions-and-tilings","title":"Constructions and Tilings","section":"Part II","summary":"We construct parallel lines and triangles with a ruler, compass and protractor, and study which shapes can cover a flat surface without gaps or overlaps.","nTerms":4},{"id":"finding-the-unknown","title":"Finding the Unknown","section":"Part II","summary":"We write simple equations from word problems and solve them by doing the same operation on both sides, then check the answer.","nTerms":4}]},"mathspb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["Mathematics (earlier NCERT book)"],"note":"PSEB 2026–27 uses the earlier NCERT book for this class; chapters follow its list.","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"integers","title":"Integers","section":"Number","summary":"Integers are the whole numbers and their negatives. This chapter shows how to add, subtract, multiply and divide them, using the number line and simple sign rules.","nTerms":5},{"id":"fractions-and-decimals","title":"Fractions and Decimals","section":"Number","summary":"Learn to multiply and divide fractions and decimals. Reciprocals and the position of the decimal point are the two big ideas.","nTerms":5},{"id":"data-handling","title":"Data Handling","section":"Data","summary":"Collect, organise and summarise data using mean, median, mode and range. Bar graphs display data, and probability measures how likely an event is.","nTerms":6},{"id":"simple-equations","title":"Simple Equations","section":"Algebra","summary":"An equation is a balance between two expressions. We solve it by doing the same thing to both sides, and we use equations to solve everyday word problems.","nTerms":6},{"id":"lines-and-angles","title":"Lines and Angles","section":"Geometry","summary":"Study pairs of angles (complementary, supplementary, linear pair, vertically opposite) and the angles made when a transversal cuts parallel lines.","nTerms":6},{"id":"triangle-and-its-properties","title":"The Triangle and its Properties","section":"Geometry","summary":"Medians, altitudes, the angle sum and exterior angle properties, the triangle inequality and the Pythagoras property of right-angled triangles.","nTerms":6},{"id":"comparing-quantities","title":"Comparing Quantities","section":"Number","summary":"Compare quantities using ratio and percentage, find profit, loss and discount in shopping, and calculate simple interest on money lent or deposited.","nTerms":6},{"id":"rational-numbers","title":"Rational Numbers","section":"Number","summary":"Rational numbers are numbers of the form p/q. We learn their standard form, how to compare them on the number line, how to operate on them and which properties they follow.","nTerms":5},{"id":"perimeter-and-area","title":"Perimeter and Area","section":"Measurement","summary":"Find the boundary length and the surface area of rectangles, squares, parallelograms, triangles and circles, and learn the units used to measure them.","nTerms":6},{"id":"algebraic-expressions","title":"Algebraic Expressions","section":"Algebra","summary":"Learn the parts of an algebraic expression, how to add and subtract like terms, how to find the value of an expression and how letters describe number patterns.","nTerms":6},{"id":"exponents-and-powers","title":"Exponents and Powers","section":"Number","summary":"Exponents are a short way of writing repeated multiplication. We learn the laws of exponents and use powers of 10 to write very large numbers in standard form.","nTerms":5},{"id":"symmetry","title":"Symmetry","section":"Geometry","summary":"A figure has line symmetry when it can be folded onto itself along a line, and rotational symmetry when it looks the same after a turn about a point.","nTerms":6},{"id":"visualising-solid-shapes","title":"Visualising Solid Shapes","section":"Geometry","summary":"Look at 3D shapes (solids): their faces, edges and vertices, the nets that fold into them, and how they look from different directions or when sliced.","nTerms":6}]},"punjabi":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ-7 (ਪਹਿਲੀ ਭਾਸ਼ਾ)"],"note":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ ਅਤੇ ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ, ਨਾਲ ਹੀ ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ-7 (ਪਹਿਲੀ ਭਾਸ਼ਾ) ਦੇ ਸਾਰੇ ਪਾਠ (ਆਪਣੇ ਸ਼ਬਦਾਂ ਵਿੱਚ ਸੰਖੇਪ)।","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"visheshan","title":"ਵਿਸ਼ੇਸ਼ਣ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਵਿਸ਼ੇਸ਼ਣ ਉਹ ਸ਼ਬਦ ਹੈ ਜੋ ਨਾਂਵ ਜਾਂ ਪੜਨਾਂਵ ਦੀ ਕੋਈ ਖ਼ਾਸੀਅਤ ਦੱਸਦਾ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਵਿਸ਼ੇਸ਼ਣ ਦੀ ਪਛਾਣ ਅਤੇ ਉਸ ਦੀਆਂ ਮੁੱਖ ਕਿਸਮਾਂ ਸਮਝਾਈਆਂ ਗਈਆਂ ਹਨ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kiriya","title":"ਕਿਰਿਆ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਕਿਰਿਆ ਵਾਕ ਦਾ ਉਹ ਸ਼ਬਦ ਹੈ ਜਿਸ ਤੋਂ ਕੰਮ ਦਾ ਕਰਨਾ ਜਾਂ ਹੋਣਾ ਪਤਾ ਲੱਗਦਾ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਕਿਰਿਆ ਦੀ ਪਛਾਣ, ਸਕਰਮਕ-ਅਕਰਮਕ ਭੇਦ ਅਤੇ ਸਹਾਇਕ ਕਿਰਿਆ ਦੱਸੀ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kaal","title":"ਕਾਲ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਕਾਲ ਤੋਂ ਪਤਾ ਲੱਗਦਾ ਹੈ ਕਿ ਕੰਮ ਕਦੋਂ ਹੋਇਆ, ਹੋ ਰਿਹਾ ਹੈ ਜਾਂ ਹੋਵੇਗਾ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਭੂਤ, ਵਰਤਮਾਨ ਅਤੇ ਭਵਿੱਖਤ ਕਾਲ ਉਦਾਹਰਨਾਂ ਸਹਿਤ ਸਮਝਾਏ ਗਏ ਹਨ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"samanarthak-shabad","title":"ਸਮਾਨਾਰਥਕ ਸ਼ਬਦ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਸਮਾਨਾਰਥਕ ਸ਼ਬਦ ਉਹ ਹਨ ਜਿਨ੍ਹਾਂ ਦੇ ਅਰਥ ਇੱਕੋ ਜਿਹੇ ਜਾਂ ਮਿਲਦੇ-ਜੁਲਦੇ ਹੋਣ। ਇਹਨਾਂ ਦੀ ਵਰਤੋਂ ਨਾਲ ਭਾਸ਼ਾ ਵਿੱਚ ਵੰਨ-ਸੁਵੰਨਤਾ ਆਉਂਦੀ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"muhavare","title":"ਮੁਹਾਵਰੇ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਮੁਹਾਵਰਾ ਅਜਿਹਾ ਵਾਕੰਸ਼ ਹੈ ਜੋ ਆਪਣੇ ਸਿੱਧੇ ਅਰਥ ਦੀ ਥਾਂ ਕੋਈ ਖ਼ਾਸ ਅਰਥ ਦਿੰਦਾ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਕੁਝ ਪ੍ਰਚਲਿਤ ਮੁਹਾਵਰੇ ਅਰਥਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਵਾਕਾਂ ਸਹਿਤ ਦਿੱਤੇ ਗਏ ਹਨ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"viram-chinh","title":"ਵਿਰਾਮ ਚਿੰਨ੍ਹ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਵਿਰਾਮ ਚਿੰਨ੍ਹ ਲਿਖਤ ਵਿੱਚ ਰੁਕਣ ਅਤੇ ਭਾਵ ਪ੍ਰਗਟ ਕਰਨ ਲਈ ਵਰਤੇ ਜਾਂਦੇ ਹਨ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੂਰਨ ਵਿਰਾਮ, ਕੌਮਾ, ਪ੍ਰਸ਼ਨ ਚਿੰਨ੍ਹ ਅਤੇ ਵਿਸਮਿਕ ਚਿੰਨ੍ਹ ਦੀ ਵਰਤੋਂ ਸਮਝਾਈ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"lekh-rachna","title":"ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ","section":"ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ","summary":"ਲੇਖ ਕਿਸੇ ਵਿਸ਼ੇ ਬਾਰੇ ਕ੍ਰਮ ਨਾਲ ਲਿਖੇ ਵਿਚਾਰ ਹੁੰਦੇ ਹਨ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਲੇਖ ਦੇ ਭਾਗ, ਲਿਖਣ ਦੇ ਢੰਗ ਅਤੇ ਇੱਕ ਨਮੂਨਾ-ਰੂਪ-ਰੇਖਾ ਦਿੱਤੀ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"daftari-chitthi","title":"ਦਫ਼ਤਰੀ ਚਿੱਠੀ (ਬਿਨੈ-ਪੱਤਰ)","section":"ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ","summary":"ਦਫ਼ਤਰੀ ਚਿੱਠੀ ਜਾਂ ਬਿਨੈ-ਪੱਤਰ ਕਿਸੇ ਅਧਿਕਾਰੀ ਜਾਂ ਮੁੱਖ ਅਧਿਆਪਕ ਨੂੰ ਕੋਈ ਬੇਨਤੀ ਕਰਨ ਲਈ ਲਿਖਿਆ ਜਾਂਦਾ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਇਸ ਦਾ ਰੂਪ ਅਤੇ ਛੁੱਟੀ ਦੀ ਅਰਜ਼ੀ ਦਾ ਨਮੂਨਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"samvad-rachna","title":"ਸੰਵਾਦ ਰਚਨਾ","section":"ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ","summary":"ਸੰਵਾਦ ਦੋ ਜਾਂ ਵੱਧ ਵਿਅਕਤੀਆਂ ਦੀ ਗੱਲਬਾਤ ਨੂੰ ਲਿਖਤ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੇਸ਼ ਕਰਨਾ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਸੰਵਾਦ ਲਿਖਣ ਦੇ ਨਿਯਮ ਅਤੇ ਇੱਕ ਨਮੂਨਾ ਦਿੱਤਾ ਗਿਆ ਹੈ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"vanjara","title":"ਵਣਜਾਰਾ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਹ ਗੀਤ ਇੱਕ ਘੁਮੰਤਰੂ ਵਣਜਾਰੇ ਦੀ ਆਵਾਜ਼ ਵਿੱਚ ਸਭ ਧਰਮਾਂ ਦੇ ਲੋਕਾਂ ਨੂੰ ਪਿਆਰ, ਏਕੇ ਅਤੇ ਭਾਈਚਾਰੇ ਦਾ ਸੁਨੇਹਾ ਦਿੰਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"moti","title":"ਮੋਤੀ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇੱਕ ਬੱਚੇ ਅਤੇ ਉਸ ਦੇ ਵਫ਼ਾਦਾਰ ਕੁੱਤੇ ਮੋਤੀ ਦੀ ਕਹਾਣੀ, ਜੋ ਪਿਆਰ, ਵਫ਼ਾਦਾਰੀ ਅਤੇ ਵਿਛੋੜੇ ਦੇ ਦੁੱਖ ਨੂੰ ਦਰਸਾਉਂਦੀ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"surinder-kaur","title":"ਪੰਜਾਬ ਦੀ ਲੋਕ-ਗਾਇਕਾ: ਸੁਰਿੰਦਰ ਕੌਰ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਜੀਵਨੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੰਜਾਬ ਦੀ ਮਸ਼ਹੂਰ ਲੋਕ-ਗਾਇਕਾ ਸੁਰਿੰਦਰ ਕੌਰ ਦੇ ਜੀਵਨ, ਸਿੱਖਿਆ, ਗੀਤਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਸੰਗੀਤ ਪ੍ਰਤੀ ਸਮਰਪਣ ਦੀ ਗੱਲ ਕੀਤੀ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"ghade-da-pani","title":"ਘੜੇ ਦਾ ਪਾਣੀ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਲੇਖ ਵਿੱਚ ਘੜੇ ਦੀ ਪੁਰਾਣੀ ਵਰਤੋਂ, ਉਸ ਦੇ ਪਾਣੀ ਦੇ ਗੁਣਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਬਦਲਦੇ ਸਮੇਂ ਵਿੱਚ ਉਸ ਦੀ ਘਟਦੀ ਥਾਂ ਦੀ ਗੱਲ ਕੀਤੀ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"maa-da-pyar","title":"ਮਾਂ ਦਾ ਪਿਆਰ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਕਵਿਤਾ ਵਿੱਚ ਮਾਂ ਦੇ ਨਿਰਸੁਆਰਥ ਪਿਆਰ, ਫ਼ਿਕਰ ਅਤੇ ਕੁਰਬਾਨੀ ਦਾ ਵਰਣਨ ਹੈ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"baldan-wala-pyara-singh","title":"ਬਲਦਾਂ ਵਾਲਾ ਪਿਆਰਾ ਸਿੰਘ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਹ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਇੱਕ ਮਿਹਨਤੀ ਕਿਸਾਨ ਪਿਆਰਾ ਸਿੰਘ ਦੀ ਹੈ, ਜੋ ਬਲਦਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਦੇਸੀ ਖਾਦ ਨਾਲ ਕੁਦਰਤੀ ਖੇਤੀ ਕਰਦਾ ਹੈ ਅਤੇ ਪਿੰਡ ਲਈ ਮਿਸਾਲ ਬਣਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"baal-khedan","title":"ਬਾਲ-ਖੇਡਾਂ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਮਨਮੋਹਨ ਸਿੰਘ ਦਾਊਂ ਦੀ ਇਸ ਕਵਿਤਾ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੰਜਾਬ ਦੀਆਂ ਰਵਾਇਤੀ ਬਾਲ-ਖੇਡਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਬੱਚਿਆਂ ਦੇ ਮਿਲ ਕੇ ਖੇਡਣ ਦੀ ਖ਼ੁਸ਼ੀ ਦਾ ਵਰਣਨ ਹੈ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"bachittar-singh-di-bahaduri","title":"ਬਚਿੱਤਰ ਸਿੰਘ ਦੀ ਬਹਾਦਰੀ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਕਾਵਿ-ਕਹਾਣੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਦੱਸਿਆ ਗਿਆ ਹੈ ਕਿ ਗੁਰੂ ਗੋਬਿੰਦ ਸਿੰਘ ਜੀ ਦੇ ਸਿੱਖ ਬਚਿੱਤਰ ਸਿੰਘ ਨੇ ਨਸ਼ੇ ਵਿੱਚ ਮਸਤ ਕੀਤੇ ਹਾਥੀ ਨੂੰ ਆਪਣੀ ਬਹਾਦਰੀ ਨਾਲ ਭਜਾ ਦਿੱਤਾ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"mere-dadi-ji","title":"ਮੇਰੇ ਦਾਦੀ ਜੀ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਗੁਰਬਖ਼ਸ਼ ਸਿੰਘ ਪ੍ਰੀਤਲੜੀ ਦੇ ਇਸ ਲੇਖ ਵਿੱਚ ਲੇਖਕ ਆਪਣੀ ਸਾਦੀ, ਧਾਰਮਿਕ, ਕੋਮਲ ਅਤੇ ਪਿਆਰ ਭਰੀ ਦਾਦੀ ਜੀ ਦਾ ਸ਼ਬਦ-ਚਿੱਤਰ ਪੇਸ਼ ਕਰਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"sheraniyan","title":"ਸ਼ੇਰਨੀਆਂ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਕੁਲਵੰਤ ਸਿੰਘ ਵਿਰਕ ਦੀ ਇਹ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਰਾਤ ਨੂੰ ਪਾਰਕ ਵਿੱਚ ਮਿਲੀਆਂ ਦੋ ਕੁੜੀਆਂ ਬਾਰੇ ਹੈ, ਜਿਨ੍ਹਾਂ ਬਾਰੇ ਬਿਨਾਂ ਜਾਣੇ ਗ਼ਲਤ ਸੋਚਣਾ ਠੀਕ ਨਹੀਂ ਹੁੰਦਾ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"baba-beda-singh-bahadur","title":"ਬਾਬਾ ਬੰਦਾ ਸਿੰਘ ਬਹਾਦਰ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਜੀਵਨੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਬਾਬਾ ਬੰਦਾ ਸਿੰਘ ਬਹਾਦਰ ਦੇ ਜੀਵਨ, ਸਰਹਿੰਦ ਦੀ ਜਿੱਤ, ਗ਼ਰੀਬਾਂ ਪ੍ਰਤੀ ਹਮਦਰਦੀ ਅਤੇ ਸ਼ਹੀਦੀ ਦਾ ਵਰਣਨ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"shabash-suman","title":"ਸ਼ਾਬਾਸ਼ ! ਸੁਮਨ !","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਸ਼ਿਮਲੇ ਦੀ ਸੈਰ ਦੌਰਾਨ ਸੁਮਨ ਪਿੰਜਰੇ ਵਿੱਚ ਬੰਦ ਤੋਤੇ ਨੂੰ ਖਰੀਦ ਕੇ ਆਜ਼ਾਦ ਕਰ ਦਿੰਦੀ ਹੈ। ਕਹਾਣੀ ਪੰਛੀਆਂ ਪ੍ਰਤੀ ਦਇਆ ਅਤੇ ਆਜ਼ਾਦੀ ਦਾ ਸੁਨੇਹਾ ਦਿੰਦੀ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"saun","title":"ਸਾਉਣ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਧਨੀ ਰਾਮ ਚਾਤ੍ਰਿਕ ਦੀ ਇਸ ਕਵਿਤਾ ਵਿੱਚ ਸਾਉਣ ਮਹੀਨੇ ਦੀ ਬਰਸਾਤ, ਹਰਿਆਲੀ ਅਤੇ ਪੇਂਡੂ ਜੀਵਨ ਦੀਆਂ ਰੌਣਕਾਂ ਦਾ ਸੋਹਣਾ ਚਿੱਤਰ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kartar-singh-sarabha","title":"ਕਰਤਾਰ ਸਿੰਘ ਸਰਾਭਾ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਜੀਵਨੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਗ਼ਦਰ ਲਹਿਰ ਦੇ ਮਹਾਨ ਨਾਇਕ ਕਰਤਾਰ ਸਿੰਘ ਸਰਾਭਾ ਦੇ ਜੀਵਨ, ਅਮਰੀਕਾ ਜਾਣ, ਦੇਸ ਪਰਤਣ ਅਤੇ ਸ਼ਹੀਦੀ ਦਾ ਵੇਰਵਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"jee-kare","title":"ਜੀਅ ਕਰੇ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਛੋਟੀ ਕਵਿਤਾ ਵਿੱਚ ਕਵੀ ਦਾ ਮਨ ਰੁੱਖ ਬਣ ਕੇ ਸਭ ਨੂੰ ਹਰਿਆਲੀ, ਛਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਪੰਛੀਆਂ ਨੂੰ ਆਲ੍ਹਣੇ ਦੇਣਾ ਚਾਹੁੰਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"trilochan-da-kad","title":"ਤ੍ਰਿਲੋਚਨ ਦਾ ਕੱਦ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਕਰਤਾਰ ਸਿੰਘ ਦੁੱਗਲ ਦੀ ਇਹ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਇੱਕ ਸੁਸਤ ਮਾਲੀ ਤ੍ਰਿਲੋਚਨ ਦੀ ਹੈ, ਜੋ ਮਾਲਕਾਂ ਦੀ ਦਿਲਚਸਪੀ ਅਤੇ ਛੱਤ ਦੇ ਬਗ਼ੀਚੇ ਨਾਲ ਮਿਹਨਤੀ ਅਤੇ ਜ਼ਿੰਮੇਵਾਰ ਬਣ ਜਾਂਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"lala-lajpat-rai","title":"ਲਾਲਾ ਲਾਜਪਤ ਰਾਏ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਬਲਵੰਤ ਗਾਰਗੀ ਦੀ ਇਸ ਜੀਵਨੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੰਜਾਬ ਕੇਸਰੀ ਲਾਲਾ ਲਾਜਪਤ ਰਾਏ ਦੇ ਬਚਪਨ, ਵਕਾਲਤ, ਦੇਸ-ਸੇਵਾ, ਕੁਰਬਾਨੀ ਅਤੇ ਸਾਈਮਨ ਕਮਿਸ਼ਨ ਵਿਰੋਧੀ ਜਲੂਸ ਦਾ ਵਰਣਨ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"gathmuthian-wala-khuh","title":"ਗਿਠਮੁਠੀਆਂ ਵਾਲਾ ਖੂਹ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਕਰਨਲ ਜਸਬੀਰ ਭੁੱਲਰ ਦੀ ਇਹ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਖ਼ਵਾਸਪੁਰ ਪਿੰਡ ਦੇ ਨਵੇਂ ਖੂਹ ਅਤੇ ਬੱਚੇ ਬੀਰ੍ਹੇ ਦੀ ਜਗਿਆਸਾ ਬਾਰੇ ਹੈ, ਜੋ ਗਿਠਮੁਠੀਆਂ ਬਾਰੇ ਫੈਲੇ ਵਹਿਮ ਦੀ ਸੱਚਾਈ ਖ਼ੁਦ ਲੱਭਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"adbhut-sansar","title":"ਅਦਭੁਤ ਸੰਸਾਰ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਕਵਿਤਾ ਵਿੱਚ ਸਮੁੰਦਰ ਹੇਠਲੇ ਅਨੋਖੇ ਜੀਵਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਜੰਗਲਾਂ ਦੀ ਦੁਨੀਆ ਦਾ ਹੈਰਾਨੀ ਭਰਿਆ ਵਰਣਨ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"satt-doctor","title":"ਸੱਤ ਡਾਕਟਰ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਗੁਰਬਚਨ ਸਿੰਘ ਭੁੱਲਰ ਦੀ ਇਹ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਸੁਖਜੋਤ ਦੀ ਹੈ, ਜੋ ਧਰਤੀ ਦੇ ਗਰਮ ਹੋਣ ਬਾਰੇ ਜਾਣ ਕੇ ਡਰ ਦੀ ਥਾਂ ਹਿੰਮਤ ਕਰਦਾ ਹੈ ਅਤੇ ਘਰ ਵਿੱਚ ਸੱਤ ਨਿੰਮ ਲਾਉਂਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"pulaar-pari-sunita-williams","title":"ਪੁਲਾੜ-ਪਰੀ: ਸੁਨੀਤਾ ਵਿਲੀਅਮਜ਼","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਜੀਵਨੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਭਾਰਤੀ ਮੂਲ ਦੀ ਪੁਲਾੜ-ਯਾਤਰੀ ਸੁਨੀਤਾ ਵਿਲੀਅਮਜ਼ ਦੇ ਬਚਪਨ, ਪੜ੍ਹਾਈ, ਮਿਹਨਤ ਅਤੇ ਪੁਲਾੜ ਵਿੱਚ ਬਣਾਏ ਰਿਕਾਰਡਾਂ ਦੀ ਗੱਲ ਕੀਤੀ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"virasat-e-khalsa","title":"ਵਿਰਾਸਤ-ਏ-ਖ਼ਾਲਸਾ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਲੇਖ ਵਿੱਚ ਸ੍ਰੀ ਅਨੰਦਪੁਰ ਸਾਹਿਬ ਵਿਖੇ ਬਣੇ ਵਿਰਾਸਤ-ਏ-ਖ਼ਾਲਸਾ ਅਜਾਇਬ-ਘਰ, ਉਸ ਦੇ ਉਦੇਸ਼, ਗੈਲਰੀਆਂ ਅਤੇ ਨਿਯਮਾਂ ਬਾਰੇ ਜਾਣਕਾਰੀ ਦਿੱਤੀ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"milkhi-da-viah","title":"ਮਿਲਖੀ ਦਾ ਵਿਆਹ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਹ ਮਜ਼ੇਦਾਰ ਕਾਵਿ-ਗੀਤ ਬਾਂਦਰ ਮਿਲਖੀ ਦੇ ਵਿਆਹ ਦੀ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਹੈ, ਜਿਸ ਨੂੰ ਆਪਣੀਆਂ ਗੰਦੀਆਂ ਆਦਤਾਂ ਕਾਰਨ ਪਤਨੀ ਦਾ ਵਿਛੋੜਾ ਝੱਲਣਾ ਪੈਂਦਾ ਹੈ ਅਤੇ ਅੰਤ ਸੁਧਰ ਕੇ ਉਹ ਉਸ ਨੂੰ ਮਨਾ ਲੈਂਦਾ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"jago","title":"ਜਾਗੋ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਕਿਰਪਾਲ ਸਿੰਘ ਕਜ਼ਾਕ ਦੇ ਇਸ ਲੇਖ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਵਿਆਹ ਦੀ ਰਸਮ ਜਾਗੋ, ਨਾਨਕਾ-ਮੇਲ, ਖਾਰੇ ਚੜ੍ਹਨ ਅਤੇ ਮਾਈਏਂ ਆਦਿ ਦਾ ਵੇਰਵਾ ਇੱਕ ਬੱਚੇ ਰਾਜੂ ਦੇ ਅਨੁਭਵ ਰਾਹੀਂ ਦਿੱਤਾ ਗਿਆ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kirat-da-satkar","title":"ਕਿਰਤ ਦਾ ਸਤਿਕਾਰ","section":"ਪੰਜਾਬੀ ਪੁਸਤਕ","summary":"ਇਸ ਇਕਾਂਗੀ ਵਿੱਚ ਇੱਕ ਮਿਹਨਤੀ ਬੂਟ-ਪਾਲਿਸ਼ ਵਾਲੇ ਵਿਦਿਆਰਥੀ ਕ੍ਰਿਸ਼ਨ ਅਤੇ ਇੱਕ ਸੇਠ ਦੇ ਵਿਹਾਰ ਰਾਹੀਂ ਹੱਥੀਂ ਕਿਰਤ ਦੇ ਸਤਿਕਾਰ ਦਾ ਸੁਨੇਹਾ ਦਿੱਤਾ ਗਿਆ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5}]},"science":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Curiosity"],"reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"the-ever-evolving-world-of-science","title":"The Ever-Evolving World of Science","section":"Nature of Science","summary":"Science is a way of understanding the world through curiosity, observation and experiments. This chapter looks at the scientific method, why scientific ideas change with new evidence, and how science and technology help us.","nTerms":5},{"id":"exploring-substances-acidic-basic-and-neutral","title":"Exploring Substances: Acidic, Basic, and Neutral","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Some substances taste sour, some feel soapy and some are neither. This chapter explains acids, bases and neutral substances, how indicators change colour to identify them, and what happens when an acid meets a base (neutralisation).","nTerms":6},{"id":"electricity-circuits-and-their-components","title":"Electricity: Circuits and their Components","section":"Physics","summary":"Electric current flows only through a complete path called a circuit. This chapter looks at cells, bulbs, LEDs and switches, circuit symbols, conductors and insulators, series and parallel connections, and how a fuse protects us.","nTerms":6},{"id":"the-world-of-metals-and-non-metals","title":"The World of Metals and Non-metals","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Most elements around us are metals or non-metals. This chapter compares their physical properties, shows how they react with oxygen, acids and salt solutions, and describes where we use them.","nTerms":6},{"id":"changes-around-us-physical-and-chemical","title":"Changes Around Us: Physical and Chemical","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Everything around us keeps changing. In a physical change no new substance is formed, while in a chemical change one or more new substances are made. This chapter covers examples of each, rusting and its prevention, and crystallisation.","nTerms":6},{"id":"adolescence-a-stage-of-growth-and-change","title":"Adolescence: A Stage of Growth and Change","section":"Biology","summary":"Adolescence is the stage of growth between childhood and adulthood. This chapter explains the changes of puberty, the role of hormones, the menstrual cycle, how the sex of a baby is determined, and how to stay healthy.","nTerms":6},{"id":"heat-transfer-in-nature","title":"Heat Transfer in Nature","section":"Physics","summary":"Heat always flows from a hotter object to a colder one. This chapter explains temperature and thermometers, the three modes of heat transfer (conduction, convection and radiation), sea and land breezes, and why we choose certain clothes and vessels.","nTerms":6},{"id":"measurement-of-time-and-motion","title":"Measurement of Time and Motion","section":"Physics","summary":"From sundials to atomic clocks, people have always measured time. This chapter covers the types of motion, the simple pendulum, speed and its units, uniform and non-uniform motion, and distance-time graphs.","nTerms":6},{"id":"life-processes-in-animals","title":"Life Processes in Animals","section":"Biology","summary":"Animals carry out life processes such as nutrition, respiration, transport and excretion to stay alive. This chapter follows food through the human digestive system and looks at breathing, blood and the heart, and how the body removes waste, with examples from other animals.","nTerms":6},{"id":"life-processes-in-plants","title":"Life Processes in Plants","section":"Biology","summary":"Plants make their own food by photosynthesis, take in water and minerals through roots, lose water through transpiration, and also respire and get rid of waste. This chapter also covers other ways in which plants get food, such as parasites and insectivorous plants.","nTerms":6},{"id":"light-shadows-and-reflections","title":"Light: Shadows and Reflections","section":"Physics","summary":"Light helps us see, travels in straight lines and forms shadows. This chapter covers luminous and non-luminous objects, transparent, translucent and opaque materials, how shadows form, the pinhole camera, and reflection from plane mirrors.","nTerms":6},{"id":"earth-moon-and-the-sun","title":"Earth, Moon, and the Sun","section":"Physics","summary":"Day and night, years and seasons, the phases of the Moon and eclipses all come from the motions of the Earth and the Moon. This chapter explains rotation and revolution, the seasons, the Moon's phases, and solar and lunar eclipses.","nTerms":6}]},"sciencepb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["Science (earlier NCERT book)"],"note":"PSEB 2026–27 uses the earlier NCERT book for this class; chapters follow its list.","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"nutrition-in-plants","title":"Nutrition in Plants","section":"Biology","summary":"Green plants make their own food by photosynthesis, while other plants and organisms obtain food in different ways. This chapter explains photosynthesis, other modes of plant nutrition and how soil nutrients are replaced.","nTerms":6},{"id":"nutrition-in-animals","title":"Nutrition in Animals","section":"Biology","summary":"Animals take in complex food and break it down into simple substances their cells can use. This chapter follows food through the human digestive system and compares digestion in grass-eaters and Amoeba.","nTerms":6},{"id":"heat","title":"Heat","section":"Physics","summary":"Temperature tells how hot or cold an object is, and heat flows from hotter to colder objects. This chapter covers thermometers and the three modes of heat transfer: conduction, convection and radiation.","nTerms":5},{"id":"acids-bases-and-salts","title":"Acids, Bases and Salts","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Substances can be grouped as acids, bases or neutral, which indicators help us tell apart. This chapter explains common acids and bases, how they react together in neutralisation to form salts, and where this is used in daily life.","nTerms":5},{"id":"physical-and-chemical-changes","title":"Physical and Chemical Changes","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Changes around us are either physical or chemical. This chapter explains how to tell them apart, then studies rusting, how it can be prevented, and crystallisation.","nTerms":5},{"id":"respiration-in-organisms","title":"Respiration in Organisms","section":"Biology","summary":"Respiration releases energy from food in every living cell. This chapter distinguishes aerobic and anaerobic respiration, describes breathing in humans and compares respiration in other animals and plants.","nTerms":5},{"id":"transportation-in-animals-and-plants","title":"Transportation in Animals and Plants","section":"Biology","summary":"Materials such as food, oxygen and waste must be moved around the body. This chapter covers blood, blood vessels and the heart, excretion in humans, and transport of water and food in plants.","nTerms":6},{"id":"reproduction-in-plants","title":"Reproduction in Plants","section":"Biology","summary":"Plants produce new plants by asexual or sexual reproduction. This chapter covers vegetative propagation, budding, fragmentation and spores, then sexual reproduction through flowers, pollination, fertilisation, fruit and seed dispersal.","nTerms":6},{"id":"motion-and-time","title":"Motion and Time","section":"Physics","summary":"Objects move at different speeds. This chapter defines speed, describes uniform and non-uniform motion, shows how time is measured with a pendulum and introduces distance-time graphs.","nTerms":5},{"id":"electric-current-and-its-effects","title":"Electric Current and its Effects","section":"Physics","summary":"Electric current has heating and magnetic effects. This chapter introduces circuit symbols, the heating effect used in fuses, heaters and bulbs, and the magnetic effect used in electromagnets and electric bells.","nTerms":5},{"id":"light","title":"Light","section":"Physics","summary":"Light travels in straight lines and bounces off surfaces. This chapter covers reflection, images in plane and spherical mirrors, lenses and the splitting of white light into colours.","nTerms":6}]},"sst":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Exploring Society: India and Beyond Part 1","Exploring Society: India and Beyond Part 2"],"reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"geographical-diversity-of-india","title":"Geographical Diversity of India","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"The major physical divisions of India: mountains, plains, plateau, desert, coasts and islands, and how each shapes life.","nTerms":4},{"id":"understanding-the-weather","title":"Understanding the Weather","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"What weather is, the elements that make it up, and how heat, pressure, wind and moisture produce rain.","nTerms":5},{"id":"climates-of-india","title":"Climates of India","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"The factors that shape India's climate, the monsoon, and the four seasons of the year.","nTerms":4},{"id":"new-beginnings-cities-and-states","title":"New Beginnings: Cities and States","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"How new cities, kingdoms and republics grew from about the 6th century BCE, and why Magadha became powerful.","nTerms":4},{"id":"the-rise-of-empires","title":"The Rise of Empires","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"The Mauryan empire: Chandragupta Maurya, its administration, and Ashoka's policy of dhamma.","nTerms":4},{"id":"the-age-of-reorganisation","title":"The Age of Reorganisation","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"How new kingdoms and trading links shaped India after the Mauryas, in the north, the Deccan and the far south.","nTerms":4},{"id":"the-gupta-era","title":"The Gupta Era: An Age of Tireless Creativity","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"The Gupta empire, its great rulers and the advances in science, mathematics, literature and art in this period.","nTerms":4},{"id":"how-the-land-becomes-sacred","title":"How the Land Becomes Sacred","section":"Our Cultural Heritage and Knowledge Traditions","summary":"How rivers, mountains, forests and places of worship came to be seen as sacred in India, and the idea of pilgrimage.","nTerms":4},{"id":"from-the-rulers-to-the-ruled","title":"From the Rulers to the Ruled: Types of Governments","section":"Governance and Democracy","summary":"Why we need governments, the main types of government, and why democracy is based on the participation of citizens.","nTerms":4},{"id":"the-constitution-of-india-an-introduction","title":"The Constitution of India — An Introduction","section":"Governance and Democracy","summary":"What a constitution is, how India's was made, and its main features: the Preamble, fundamental rights, directive principles and duties.","nTerms":5},{"id":"from-barter-to-money","title":"From Barter to Money","section":"Economic Life Around Us","summary":"How trade began with barter, why money replaced it, and how money has changed from coins to digital payments.","nTerms":4},{"id":"understanding-markets","title":"Understanding Markets","section":"Economic Life Around Us","summary":"Different kinds of markets, how goods travel from producer to consumer, and how demand and supply affect prices.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-story-of-indian-farming","title":"The Story of Indian Farming","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"Farming seasons and crops in India, types of farming, irrigation, and how Indian agriculture has changed.","nTerms":5},{"id":"india-and-her-neighbours","title":"India and Her Neighbours","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"India's neighbouring countries, their geography, shared history and ties, and why good relations matter.","nTerms":4},{"id":"empires-and-kingdoms-6th-to-10th-centuries","title":"Empires and Kingdoms: 6th to 10th Centuries","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"Major kingdoms of the 6th to 10th centuries, including Harsha, the Chalukyas, Pallavas, Rashtrakutas and Palas, and their art and learning.","nTerms":4},{"id":"turning-tides-11th-and-12th-centuries","title":"Turning Tides: 11th and 12th Centuries","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"The Cholas at their peak, raids from the north-west, and the rise of regional kingdoms in the 11th and 12th centuries.","nTerms":4},{"id":"india-a-home-to-many","title":"India, a Home to Many","section":"Our Cultural Heritage and Knowledge Traditions","summary":"India's diversity of languages, religions, food and festivals, and how many communities came to make it their home.","nTerms":4},{"id":"the-state-the-government-and-you","title":"The State, the Government, and You","section":"Governance and Democracy","summary":"The difference between the state and the government, the three organs of government, and how state and local governments work.","nTerms":5},{"id":"infrastructure-engine-of-indias-development","title":"Infrastructure: Engine of India's Development","section":"Economic Life Around Us","summary":"What infrastructure is, its main types (transport, power, communication, social), and why it matters for development.","nTerms":4},{"id":"banks-and-the-magic-of-finance","title":"Banks and the Magic of Finance","section":"Economic Life Around Us","summary":"How banks keep savings safe, give loans and earn interest, and how to use banks, cards and insurance sensibly.","nTerms":5}]},"sstpb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["Social Science (PSEB, Class 7)"],"note":"Follows the PSEB 2026–27 syllabus; written from the syllabus topics, not the PSEB book's own text.","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"environment","title":"Environment","section":"Geography","summary":"What the environment is, its natural and human parts, and the four domains of the Earth: lithosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere and biosphere.","nTerms":4},{"id":"internal-external-earth","title":"The Internal and External Face of the Earth","section":"Geography","summary":"The layers inside the Earth, the three kinds of rocks, how soils form, and the uses of minerals and mineral oil.","nTerms":5},{"id":"atmosphere-temperature","title":"Atmosphere and Temperature","section":"Geography","summary":"The layers and gases of the atmosphere, and how temperature, weather, winds and rainfall differ from place to place.","nTerms":5},{"id":"oceans","title":"Oceans","section":"Geography","summary":"The major oceans, their movements (waves, currents and tides) and the danger of tsunamis.","nTerms":4},{"id":"natural-vegetation-wildlife","title":"Natural Vegetation and Wildlife","section":"Geography","summary":"The main types of natural vegetation, the animals that live in each, and why forests and wildlife must be protected.","nTerms":4},{"id":"human-environment","title":"Human Environment: Settlements, Transport, Communication and Trade","section":"Geography","summary":"How people settle, travel, send messages and exchange goods.","nTerms":4},{"id":"india-and-the-world","title":"India and the World: When, Where and How","section":"History","summary":"How historians study the past, what sources they use, and why the medieval period of Indian history is studied.","nTerms":4},{"id":"new-kings-kingdoms","title":"New Kings and Kingdoms (700–1200 CE)","section":"History","summary":"The rise of the Rajput kingdoms, their rivalry in the north, and the early Turkish invasions.","nTerms":4},{"id":"south-india-700-1200","title":"Political Developments in South India (700–1200)","section":"History","summary":"The Cholas, Pallavas and Chalukyas, their rule, temples and village administration.","nTerms":4},{"id":"delhi-sultanate","title":"The Delhi Sultanate","section":"History","summary":"The five dynasties that ruled from Delhi between 1206 and 1526, their well-known rulers, administration and fall.","nTerms":4},{"id":"mughal-empire","title":"The Mughal Empire","section":"History","summary":"The Mughal emperors from Babur to Aurangzeb, their policies and administration.","nTerms":4},{"id":"architecture","title":"Architecture","section":"History","summary":"Temples, forts, mosques and tombs built in medieval India and the styles they show.","nTerms":4},{"id":"towns-traders-craftsmen","title":"Towns, Traders and Craftsmen","section":"History","summary":"Temple towns, capital cities, ports, crafts and trade in medieval India.","nTerms":4},{"id":"tribes-nomads-settled","title":"Tribes, Nomads and Settled Communities","section":"History","summary":"Tribal societies, nomadic pastoralists and how they related to settled rule in medieval India.","nTerms":4},{"id":"religious-developments","title":"Religious Developments","section":"History","summary":"The Bhakti movement, the Sufi tradition and the teachings of Guru Nanak Dev Ji.","nTerms":4},{"id":"regional-cultures","title":"Development of Regional Cultures","section":"History","summary":"How languages, literature, music, dance, painting and food developed in different regions of medieval India.","nTerms":4},{"id":"india-18th-century","title":"India in the 18th Century","section":"History","summary":"The decline of the Mughals and the rise of new powers: Marathas, Sikhs, Nawabs, and the arrival of the East India Company.","nTerms":4},{"id":"democracy-equality","title":"Democracy and Equality","section":"Civics","summary":"What democracy means, why equality matters, and how equality is protected in India through universal adult franchise.","nTerms":4},{"id":"democracy-representation","title":"Democracy: Institutional Representation","section":"Civics","summary":"How people are represented through elected bodies at the local, state and national levels, and how elections work.","nTerms":4},{"id":"state-government","title":"State Government","section":"Civics","summary":"How a state is governed, with the Punjab Vidhan Sabha, MLAs, the Chief Minister and the Governor as the example.","nTerms":4},{"id":"mass-communication-democracy","title":"Mass Communication and Democracy","section":"Civics","summary":"The role of newspapers, radio, television and the internet in a democracy, and the need for responsible use.","nTerms":4}]}});
